<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch03_P03_Ex8_Squares_implication</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Conjecture: for all real numbers \(x\) and \(y\), if \(x^2\gt y^2\) then \(x\gt y\). (In symbols, \(x^2\gt y^2\Rightarrow x\gt y\).)</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Give a counterexample.</p>
<div>\(x=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] \(y=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Alex wrote this disproof:</p>
<blockquote><p>Take \(x=1\) and \(y=2\). Then \(x\gt y\) is false, because \(1\lt 2\). So the statement is false.</p></blockquote>
<p>What is wrong with Alex's disproof?</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The statement is an implication: <q>if \(x^2\gt y^2\) then \(x\gt y\)</q>. A counterexample to an implication has two parts. It must make the condition \(x^2\gt y^2\) true, and it must make the conclusion \(x\gt y\) false.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Take \(x=-2\) and \(y=1\). Then \(x^2=4\gt 1=y^2\), so the condition holds, but \(x=-2\lt 1=y\), so \(x\gt y\) is false. The implication fails for this pair, and the statement is false. The counterexamples are exactly the pairs with \(x\lt -|y|\): \(x\) must be negative, and further from \(0\) than \(y\).</p></li>
<li><p>Alex's pair does not satisfy the condition: \(1^2\gt 2^2\) is false. The statement makes no claim at all about pairs with \(x^2\le y^2\), so Alex's pair cannot be a counterexample, even though \(x\gt y\) is false for it. Alex checked only the second part of a counterexample, not the first.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>A true statement nearby is \(x^2\gt y^2\Rightarrow |x|\gt |y|\).</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
